    "explanation": "To the rider and the driver — this case goes to a human reviewer, and the $4.20 in dispute is held rather than finally collected or refunded. Here is what the record does and does not show.\n\nTHE DEVIATION IS ESTABLISHED. The recommended route was 9.6 km; the trip was driven at 10.9 km, a deviation of 1.30 km (10.9 − 9.6), 13.5% over the recommended route and above the 1.0 km tolerance in POL-RD-22. The trip record shows the vehicle leaving the recommended route at the KPE/MacPherson Road exit at 21:22:26 and rejoining at Hougang Avenue 3/Upper Paya Lebar Road at 21:34:06. The distance is measured on the platform's recorded distances, not on the sparse GPS trace (9.226 km), exactly as POL-RD-22 requires. So no 'within tolerance' outcome is available.\n\nTHE REROUTE WAS TRAFFIC-JUSTIFIED (POL-RD-23). The platform-retrieved LTA advisory EV-ADVIS-05014-01 records an ACCIDENT on the KPE towards TPE after the MacPherson Road exit, valid 21:06–22:16, which covers the trip window (21:16–21:38). The advisory is on the recommended route (the KPE), in the direction of travel (towards TPE), at a point ('after MacPherson Road exit') on the stretch the recommended route continues along — where the driver diverted. I made that intersection determination from the advisory's own road, direction and location, as POL-RD-23 directs. The rider's argument that justification fails because the derived block leaves intersects_route null is therefore rejected: the clause asks the decision-maker to decide the intersection, and on these fields the advisory intersects the route.\n\nNOTICE WAS GIVEN (POL-RD-21). The driver messaged at 21:19:56 (MSG-05014-003, briefly stating the reason), the rider read it at 21:20:23, and the deviation began at 21:22:26 — before the point of deviation, in-app, so no call recording is needed. The impairment on EV-CHAT-05014 attaches to the rider's reply, not to this notice.\n\nAGREEMENT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS WHAT SENDS THE CASE TO REVIEW. POL-RD-24 row (a), which produces no refund, needs the rider to have agreed; row (b) (refund) needs her not to have been informed, which is not the case; row (d) needs the reroute not to be traffic-justified, which it is. POL-RD-21 treats a deviation as agreed only where the chat log confirms the rider's acknowledgment. The only record of it is MSG-05014-004 ('aiyo can meh... ok lor whatever u say but dun charge me more ah'), and the chat log EV-CHAT-05014 is impaired — issue code LANGUAGE_AMBIGUITY, weight_multiplier 0.85 — because it mixes assent with a condition on the fare. Whether it acknowledges or objects to the reroute is exactly what cannot be resolved. The driver's reading of it as agreement and the rider's reading of it as an objection are both plausible; the record does not choose between them. So no row of POL-RD-24 fits, and the case is escalated under POL-GEN-26 as POL-RD-24 itself provides ('informed but the rider objected').\n\nCONFIDENCE. This file requires 0.95 because the rider's profile carries a fraud flag; POL-GEN-22 raises the required confidence whatever the value, and the flag only raises the bar — it never decides the trip (POL-GEN-01). The agreement fact is carried only by the chat log at 0.85, so confidence on it cannot exceed 0.85 (POL-GEN-02(2)). Even if the reply were read as agreement, a NO_ACTION under row (a) could not be entered below the 0.95 bar (POL-RD-25). On either reading the outcome is the same: human review.\n\nTHE MONEY. The rider asks for $4.20. The figure this policy can produce is the excess distance fare: 1.30 km × 0.72 SGD/km = $0.94 (POL-RD-24; the fare ledger's own derived block records $0.94), and the driver is right that $4.20 is not a sum the matrix can generate. But no money issues now: on an escalation the amount is held and neither refunded nor finally collected (POL-GEN-03).\n\nREFERRALS: none. No clause in this file produces one — the goodwill-credit, repeat-no-show and fee-amount clauses (POL-NS-24, POL-NS-25, POL-NS-22) do not apply to a route-deviation dispute.\n\nOpen questions for the reviewer, which belong here rather than in the referrals list: whether the rider's reply amounts to agreement to the reroute (in which case POL-RD-24 row (a) applies and no refund issues), and confirmation of the traffic advisory's intersection with the recommended route. The rider's fraud flag on profile is a matter for the reviewer."
